## Why This Is Asked
This checks real-world ability to design multi-cloud patterns with clean module boundaries and governance.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform modules, provider aliases, multi-cloud strategy
- Environment backends and per-environment isolation
- CI gates, version constraints, governance for cloud support

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "aws"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  region  = var.aws_region
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "google"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  project = var.gcp_project
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "azure"
  version  = "~> 3.0"
  features {}
}
module "monitors" {
  source       = "./modules/monitors"
  cloud_config = {
    aws   = { region = var.aws_region, enabled = true }
    gcp   = { region = var.gcp_region, enabled = true }
    azure = { region = var.azure_region, enabled = true }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift in this pattern?
- How would you extend to add a new cloud while keeping tight approvals?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical module design and basic policy; it checks input validation, derived values, and CI gates.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform module input validation
- Local/derived values
- Regex-based naming conventions
- Basic GitHub Actions gates

## Code Example
```terraform
variable "env" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment: dev|staging|prod"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","staging","prod"], var.env)
    error_message = "env must be dev, staging, or prod"
  }
}

variable "bucket_name" {
  type    = string
  default = ""
}

resource "random_id" "suffix" { byte_length = 4 }

locals {
  derived_bucket = var.bucket_name != "" ? var.bucket_name : "${var.env}-app-${random_id.suffix.hex}"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = local.derived_bucket
  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm = "AES256"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate this with a per-env backend and a separate plan stage for prod-only changes?
- How would you extend validation to enforce unique bucket names across multiple regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of conditional Terraform blocks, ensuring safe incremental changes and plan stability when toggling a feature flag.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic blocks in Terraform
- Conditional resource configuration
- AWS S3 server-side encryption basics
- Plan drift and backward compatibility

## Code Example
```javascript
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "b" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = var.enable_sse ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support SSE with a customer-managed key (KMS) via kms_master_key_id?
- What tests would you add to validate idempotency when toggling the flag across environments?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of module composition, workspace-based state, and simple CI gates in a beginner-friendly way. It ensures the candidate can wire a wrapper for consistent tagging across environments and validate constraints early in CI.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform modules and wrappers
- Variable validation for environment constraints
- Workspaces and workspace-aware backends
- Per-env state isolation with a single backend
- GitHub Actions gates around terraform plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/variables.tf
variable "environment" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment name (dev, prod)"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be 'dev' or 'prod'"
  }
}
variable "cost_center" {
  type = string
}
```

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/main.tf
locals {
  common_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your expertise in multi-account AWS infrastructure management using Terraform, testing your ability to handle complex scenarios involving provider configuration, cross-account networking, state management, policy enforcement, and automated testing.

## Key Concepts
- **Provider aliases**: Enable managing multiple AWS accounts within the same Terraform configuration
- **assume_role**: Secure cross-account access using IAM role assumption
- **remote_state**: Access outputs from other Terraform state files
- **Cross-account VPC peering**: Establish network connectivity between VPCs in different AWS accounts
- **Policy as code**: Implement governance using Sentinel or OPA policies
- **CI testing**: Validate infrastructure changes using Terratest

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "shared"
  region = "us-east-1"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/TerraformRole"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "shared_vpc" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "shared-infrastructure-state"
    key    = "vpc/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection" "prod_to_shared" {
  provider        = aws.prod
  vpc_id        = aws_vpc.prod.id
  peer_vpc_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.vpc_id
  peer_account_id = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.account_id
  auto_accept   = false
  tags = {
    Name        = "prod-to-shared-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
    Environment = "production"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection_accepter" "shared_accepter" {
  provider                  = aws.shared
  vpc_peering_connection_id = aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.id
  auto_accept               = true
  tags = {
    Name        = "shared-accept-prod-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
  }
}

# Sentinel policy example
policy "vpc_peering_policy" {
  description = "Enforce DNS resolution and CostCenter tagging for VPC peering"

  enforce {
    condition = all {
      aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.enable_dns_support
      contains(aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.tags["CostCenter"], "engineering")
    }
  }
}
```

## CI Test (Terratest)
```go
tfunc TestVPCCrossAccountPeering(t *testing.T) {
  terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
    TerraformDir: "./",
    Vars: map[string]interface{}{
      "environment": "test",
    },
  }

  planOutput := terraform.InitAndPlan(t, terraformOptions)

  assert.Contains(t, planOutput, "aws_vpc_peering_connection")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group_rule")

  planJSON := terraform.PlanJson(t, terraformOptions)

  peeringResources := planJSON.Configuration.RootModule.Resources
  assert.Equal(t, 1, len(peeringResources))

  for _, resource := range peeringResources {
    assert.Equal(t, "aws_vpc_peering_connection", resource.Type)
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates enterprise-grade Terraform practices with proper separation of concerns, governance, and automated validation.
